2026-09-26 — K11h: classify frozen one-slot accounting tolerance.

Minimal control creates 8 live child memcgs on each of 4 CPUs, pins each charge,
touches/unmaps one page and waits for the child. Groups remain live while their
parent's memory.current and complete memory.stat snapshot are read. This does
not change the upstream test, increase its margin, or add a retry/sleep to it.
Fixed sealed GCC14 static O2; standalone source -Wall -Wextra -Werror, existing
cgroup_util helper. Same retained kernel pair and unchanged scratch as K11g.

                         GCC           EvoCC
memory.current          7327744       7430144
anon+file+kernel+sock     421888        421888
difference              6905856       7008256
frozen one-slot bound   1048576       1048576
seven-slot capacity     7340032       7340032

Each measurement stays unchanged over four one-second charged observations
and three after explicit memory.reclaim=1. Reclaim returns success but does not
remove this difference: it is not a proposed fix. Both diagnostic assertions
(reproduce a difference beyond the old bound), boots and shutdowns PASS.
This is test-bound classification, not original kselftest acceptance.

Source facts: mm/memcontrol.c NR_MEMCG_STOCK=7 and refill_stock retain separate
64-page caches per CPU. Frozen test_kmem.c MAX_VMSTAT_ERROR assumes only
4096*64*get_nprocs(). The existing original subject difference 1048576 is
rejected by its strict < check; reference passes that case. Earlier original
paired observations also exceed the one-slot bound transiently. A matched
upstream review explicitly identifies the missing slot-count factor:
https://lists.openwall.net/linux-kernel/2026/04/15/721
No reviewed upstream patch is applied to the frozen kernel or test.

Do not merge this with K11g's LRU folio references or test_percpu_basic's
missing slab contribution. Original pair remains GCC inner 4 PASS/2 FAIL,
EvoCC 3 PASS/3 FAIL; see cgroup-stock-displacement.out for original replay.
No historical totals, final composition or broader memory-correctness claim.

